<blockquote>
<p>but a lure who has so obviously been debilitated isn't very good bait.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's the main reason the &quot;voiceless&quot; weren't aware of the fact that their voices were taken. I was thinking of writing a Tale that tied into this article, where the boxes whisper <em>&quot;Sing with us. Sing with us. Forever. Forever&#8230;&quot;</em> before taking the voice of a D Class. My idea was that the music was so overwhelmingly beautiful and cleansing that those who heard it would seem to be eternally happy because of it, and it would raise the curiosity of others.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>What if they aren't being let go on purpose? The front office could be the boxes' larder. They &quot;eat&quot; their favorite part, the voice, first; then set the rest aside for later.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's actually a great idea!</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Problem is, I really don't know how you could convey that in the article</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Unfortunately, same here. I'll do some revisions. Thanks for the feedback!</p>

